{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction","id":986235,"title":"Positive Portrayals of Christianity in Genre Fiction","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-12T11:17:13-07:00","id":986235,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Positive Portrayals of Christianity in Genre Fiction","content":"((Spin off from the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/979988-any-conservative-gamers-around-yes-we-do-exist\">Any conservative gamers around? Yes... we do exist</a>))<br /> <br /> It was mentioned in the thread linked above that Christianity is one of Genre fiction's favorite whipping boy. Now we could continue to chew that topic to death or focus on positive portrayals of Christianity in Sci-Fi and Fantasy. Here is a couple I thought off the top of my head. I'll add more later on.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Grave-Peril-Dresden-Files-Book-ebook/dp/B001650UDA/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476295944&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=grave+peril\" target=\"_blank\">Jim Butcher's Dresden File</a>s - Michael Carpenter is a supporting character in this Fantasy Noir series (The link is to the first book he appears in). He is a Knight of the Cross, a paladin wielding a sword forged with a nail of crucifixion. The best way to describe Micheal is he is a &quot;righteous&quot; man, not a &quot;self-righteous&quot; man. Michael isn't perfect but is considered by Harry Dresden to be the best man he knows.<br /> <br /> Mary Doria Russell's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Sparrow-Novel-Mary-Doria-Russell-ebook/dp/B000SEIFGO/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476295990&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=the+sparrow+by+mary+doria+russell\" target=\"_blank\">The Sparrow</a> and <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B0012D1D9Q/ref=series_rw_dp_sw\" target=\"_blank\">Children of God</a> - A Jesuit Mission to make first contact with Intelligent Aliens goes wrong. Note, the mission goes wrong because of the difficulties inherent to first contact rather than anything having to do with religious aspect of the mission. <br /> <br /> Anyone got any others?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T11:26:33-07:00","id":986244,"title":"","content":"I got just three, and the second and third aren't fantasy/sci-fi:<br /> <br /> First, already named above, Michael Carpenter<br /> <br /> Second, Atticus Finch<br /> <br /> Third, <a href=\"http://mash.wikia.com/wiki/Father_John_Patrick_Francis_Mulcahy\" target=\"_blank\">Captain John Francis Patrick Mulcahy</a><br /> <br /> After that, I don't see a lot of positive, but a lot of that is my own bias.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":375,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","user":"Papa Bear","date":". 10-12-2016, 02:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T11:35:47-07:00","id":986254,"title":"","content":"C.S. Lewis' <i>Chronicles of Narnia </i>series is a strong example of Christian parable translated through traditional high fantasy. Many of his other books, such as <i>The Screwtape Letters, </i>take complicated questions of theology and make them entertaining and compelling. He also wrote a sci-fi trilogy called <i>The Space Trilogy,</i> but I haven't read it.<br /> <br /> You could argue that <i>Lord of the Rings</i>, the granddaddy of high fantasy, is also deeply steeped in Christian thought.<br /> <br /> Beloved children's sci-fantasy novel <i>A Wrinkle in Time </i>also has strong Christian undercurrents. She considered herself a successor to C.S. Lewis from what I remember.<br /> <br /> I think a big part of the problem is that for the past couple of decades, a lot of people who want to write explicitly Christian fiction target their audience to explicitly Christian audiences, creating an insulated sub-genre. I'm sure there are plenty of books that promote positive Christian values without being explicitly about Christianity, in the same way that most sci-fi and fantasy isn't explicitly atheist. Just because a book isn't <i>about </i>Christianity doesn't mean it doesn't promote Christian values or ideals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8492,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","user":"dxanders","date":". 10-12-2016, 02:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T11:37:44-07:00","id":986258,"title":"","content":"All of Classic World of Darkness proposes Christianity as true, with the exception of Werewolf where it is explicitly a lie, and Mage where <i>everything</i> is true.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;There is a remedy for everything but death, a hope for everything but wickedness, and everything will lapse except righteousness.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6042-manusdomine","name":"ManusDomine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6042_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T11:47:57-07:00","id":986265,"title":"","content":"Anne Rice, who is in lots of ways the godmother of urban fantasy, modern horror, and all the other building blocks of the World/Chronicle of Darkness has an interesting and complicated relationship with Christianity. I'd argue you can see her understanding of Catholic vice and virtue prominently in a lot of her earlier works, even though she didn't identify as a church member at the time. She returned to religion in the late 90s and wrote a couple of books explicitly about the life of Jesus Christ in the 2000s. She's since left the church but still considers herself a follower of Christ who can't stand behind the values that are promoted by the Catholic Church.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T11:49:04-07:00","id":986269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ManusDomine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986258#post986258\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All of Classic World of Darkness proposes Christianity as true, with the exception of Werewolf where it is explicitly a lie, and Mage where <i>everything</i> is true.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure I'd call that a positive portrayal of Christianity though. The nature and agendas of that God were left largely ambiguous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T11:50:35-07:00","id":986272,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986254#post986254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think a big part of the problem is that for the past couple of decades, a lot of people who want to write explicitly Christian fiction target their audience to explicitly Christian audiences, creating an insulated sub-genre. I'm sure there are plenty of books that promote positive Christian values without being explicitly about Christianity, in the same way that most sci-fi and fantasy isn't explicitly atheist. Just because a book isn't <i>about </i>Christianity doesn't mean it doesn't promote Christian values or ideals.</div> </div> </div> </div>I did consider adding the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left_Behind\" target=\"_blank\">Left Behind</a> series to my list. I didn't because I haven't read the series myself. Considering how many of my Christian friends have read the series, I assume it has a positive portrayal of Christianity. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T11:58:58-07:00","id":986280,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Exorcist-Ellen-Burstyn/dp/B007HVBW2A/ref=sr_1_1?s=movies-tv&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476297675&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=the+exorcist\" target=\"_blank\"><b>The Exorcist</b></a> features conflicted Priests but the righteousness and holiness of God is on display. I feel like any storyline borrowing from this film generally showcases the Priests as Good Guys. Similarly most stories featuring demonic possession that can be combated by Faith. This probably isn't exhaustive (for instance, the TV show <i>Supernatural</i> is not kind to any sort of divinity).<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Tales-Abyss-Nintendo-3DS/dp/B002I08WVY/ref=sr_1_1?s=instant-video&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476297741&amp;sr=8-1&amp;keywords=tales%2Bof%2Bthe%2Babyss&amp;th=1\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Tales of the Abyss</b></a> features The Order of Lorelei, which I feel is basically Fantasy Catholicism, complete with Saint equivalents. It's generally full of well-meaning figures, even if you are against them during some points.<br /> <br /> Speaking of quirky JRPGs, <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Radiata-Stories-PlayStation-2/dp/B0009A4EUI/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476298719&amp;sr=8-1&amp;keywords=Radiata+Stories\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Radiata Stories</b></a> features a large fantasy Church with Bishops and Archbishops and Priests, and the High Priest, Kain, is a cool bro who you can recruit later on. The church is mostly made up of people who just want to help out (and naturally has most of the healers, both NPC and party members). There's a greedy faction within the church but even they aren't really bad guys, just mercenary.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/127887-doing-first-tongue-translations?p=484226#post484226\" target=\"_blank\">Nihhina kalekal-zidu kal masun, kal manudanadu. Nihhina kalekal-zidu nukal shaghu-desasudu \u2014 nihhina kalekal-zidu kal innu-desasudu udhkal samm</a>.&quot; Arthur Ashe<br /> She/her, contributing writer for <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/363404/Scion-Pride-2021\" target=\"_blank\">Scion: Pride 2021</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1923-bigdamnhero","name":"BigDamnHero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1923_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1263"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T11:59:09-07:00","id":986281,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wonderandawe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986272#post986272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I did consider adding the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left_Behind\" target=\"_blank\">Left Behind</a> series to my list. I didn't because I haven't read the series myself. Considering how many of my Christian friends have read the series, I assume it has a positive portrayal of Christianity.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was intentionally leaving out <i>Left Behind </i>and books like it, but there should be plenty of explicitly Christian sci-fi and fantasy fiction. It's become its own sub-genre, and therein lies the rub.<br /> <br /> If you want books that speak explicitly about Christianity (and speak in an explicitly positive context), then you aren't talking about popular fiction. You're talking about sub-genre fiction. Those books are written to the sensibilities of a smaller crowd than the already niche community.<br /> <br /> For most genre fiction, you have to read between the lines. A book might promote Christian values without being explicitly Christian, and there's nothing wrong with that. That said, I do think fantasy and sci-fi may skew a little more towards a humanist/agnostic/atheist perspective simply due to the history of the genre. They rose to prominence during a time when Christianity was the de facto standard of society. As such, the format gave authors the opportunity to be transgressive and counter-cultural, to talk about issues they had with the contemporary world in a way that didn't openly denounce Christianity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T12:00:27-07:00","id":986283,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wonderandawe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986272#post986272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I did consider adding the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left_Behind\" target=\"_blank\">Left Behind</a> series to my list. I didn't because I haven't read the series myself. Considering how many of my Christian friends have read the series, I assume it has a positive portrayal of Christianity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends how one defines &quot;positive&quot;.<br /> <br /> If by positive, you mean the unambiguous designated protagonists, yes.<br /> <br /> If by positive, you mean a good example of the faith that you can take beyond its particular series - or a good <i>book</i> at all - then no. It's demeaning and patronizing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T12:01:14-07:00","id":986284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BigDamnHero</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986280#post986280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Exorcist-Ellen-Burstyn/dp/B007HVBW2A/ref=sr_1_1?s=movies-tv&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476297675&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=the+exorcist\" target=\"_blank\"><b>The Exorcist</b></a> features conflicted Priests but the righteousness and holiness of God is on display. I feel like any storyline borrowing from this film generally showcases the Priests as Good Guys. Similarly most stories featuring demonic possession that can be combated by Faith. This probably isn't exhaustive (for instance, the TV show <i>Supernatural</i> is not kind to any sort of divinity).<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Tales-Abyss-Nintendo-3DS/dp/B002I08WVY/ref=sr_1_1?s=instant-video&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476297741&amp;sr=8-1&amp;keywords=tales%2Bof%2Bthe%2Babyss&amp;th=1\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Tales of the Abyss</b></a> features The Order of Lorelei, which I feel is basically Fantasy Catholicism, complete with Saint equivalents. It's generally full of well-meaning figures, even if you are against them during some points.<br /> <br /> Speaking of quirky JRPGs, <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Radiata-Stories-PlayStation-2/dp/B0009A4EUI/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476298719&amp;sr=8-1&amp;keywords=Radiata+Stories\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Radiata Stories</b></a> features a large fantasy Church with Bishops and Archbishops and Priests, and the High Priest, Kain, is a cool bro who you can recruit later on. The church is mostly made up of people who just want to help out (and naturally has most of the healers, both NPC and party members). There's a greedy faction within the church but even they aren't really bad guys, just mercenary.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'd say pulp fantasy in general has the tendency to promote the Church as a positive force, even if it's metaphorical. Dungeons and Dragons clerics, healers and priests in video game RPGs, etc. all tend to pull recognizable aspects of the Christian church into their portrayals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T12:14:51-07:00","id":986293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jachra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986283#post986283\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Depends how one defines &quot;positive&quot;.<br /> <br /> If by positive, you mean the unambiguous designated protagonists, yes.<br /> <br /> If by positive, you mean a good example of the faith that you can take beyond its particular series - or a good <i>book</i> at all - then no. It's demeaning and patronizing.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I never read the book and just looked at the wikipedia article. Considering the pope in the book was raptured because &quot;he came to see the truth in the teachings of Martian Luther&quot;, I'll leave it off the list.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T12:17:26-07:00","id":986295,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986284#post986284\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I'd say pulp fantasy in general has the tendency to promote the Church as a positive force, even if it's metaphorical. Dungeons and Dragons clerics, healers and priests in video game RPGs, etc. all tend to pull recognizable aspects of the Christian church into their portrayals.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but evil clerics and priests exist in a lot of those things. My favorite D&amp;D setting, Eberron, explicitly allows Evil members of Good-aligned religions and churches to still use healing and good-aligned spells. I tried to find examples of Catholic- or at least Christian-esque organizations that used their political power for generally good things and weren't the primary antagonists (though allowing for high-ranking members of those churches to be bad eggs). Though I guess D&amp;D editions before 4 do require Paladins to be Lawful Good or they lose their powers, so I guess any organization of Paladins from those editions qualify as positive portrayals. So long as their leader isn't secretly a Baalor or something.<br /> <br /> The <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Castlevania-Lords-Shadow-Collection-Playstation-3/dp/B00ENWZAHO/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476299693&amp;sr=8-1&amp;keywords=castlevania%2Blords%2Bof%2Bshadow&amp;th=1\" target=\"_blank\">Castlevania: Lords of Shadow</a> franchise is <i>super</i> Catholic. Even though the main character is not a good guy, the righteousness of God is unquestioned (though it gets goofy as hell in 2). The bad guy is literally the Devil and Gabriel basically has to beat him by sincerely proclaiming his faith in the everlasting forgiveness of the Lord.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/127887-doing-first-tongue-translations?p=484226#post484226\" target=\"_blank\">Nihhina kalekal-zidu kal masun, kal manudanadu. Nihhina kalekal-zidu nukal shaghu-desasudu \u2014 nihhina kalekal-zidu kal innu-desasudu udhkal samm</a>.&quot; Arthur Ashe<br /> She/her, contributing writer for <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/363404/Scion-Pride-2021\" target=\"_blank\">Scion: Pride 2021</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1923-bigdamnhero","name":"BigDamnHero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1923_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1263"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:04:49-07:00","id":986326,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wonderandawe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986293#post986293\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I never read the book and just looked at the wikipedia article. Considering the pope in the book was raptured because &quot;he came to see the truth in the teachings of Martian Luther&quot;, I'll leave it off the list.</div> </div> </div> </div>I want to read more about this Martian Luther.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BigDamnHero</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986295#post986295\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sure, but evil clerics and priests exist in a lot of those things. My favorite D&amp;D setting, Eberron, explicitly allows Evil members of Good-aligned religions and churches to still use healing and good-aligned spells. I tried to find examples of Catholic- or at least Christian-esque organizations that used their political power for generally good things and weren't the primary antagonists (though allowing for high-ranking members of those churches to be bad eggs). Though I guess D&amp;D editions before 4 do require Paladins to be Lawful Good or they lose their powers, so I guess any organization of Paladins from those editions qualify as positive portrayals. So long as their leader isn't secretly a Baalor or something.<br /> <br /> The <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Castlevania-Lords-Shadow-Collection-Playstation-3/dp/B00ENWZAHO/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1476299693&amp;sr=8-1&amp;keywords=castlevania%2Blords%2Bof%2Bshadow&amp;th=1\" target=\"_blank\">Castlevania: Lords of Shadow</a> franchise is <i>super</i> Catholic. Even though the main character is not a good guy, the righteousness of God is unquestioned (though it gets goofy as hell in 2). The bad guy is literally the Devil and Gabriel basically has to beat him by sincerely proclaiming his faith in the everlasting forgiveness of the Lord.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but I think &quot;good paladins&quot; and &quot;good clerics&quot; that resemble members of the traditional church are more or less baked into these kinds of generic settings, and the inversion of that is the exception rather than the rule.<br /> <br /> Castlevania is a good one, btw. It has a very stark view of morality.<br /> <br /> I'd throw Daredevil in to the mix as well. Definitely a positive (if conflicted) character with an explicitly Catholic mindset.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8492,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","user":"dxanders","date":". 10-12-2016, 04:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:10:20-07:00","id":986329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986326#post986326\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I want to read more about this Martian Luther.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ha! Hilarious misspelling strikes again! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:14:10-07:00","id":986332,"title":"","content":"I'd say, even if he isn't explicitly stated as such, Peter Parker (Spider-Man) tends to be a good Protestant boy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:23:38-07:00","id":986341,"title":"","content":"Shepperd book<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPXuy2i4Mdo\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPXuy2i4Mdo\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPXuy2i4Mdo\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oPXuy2i4Mdo </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:31:06-07:00","id":986348,"title":"","content":"I would go so far as to say that fundamentalist takes on Christianity like those espoused in <i>Left Behind </i>are <i>why </i>the religion as a whole gets a bad rap in genre fiction.<br /> <br /> That, and most westerners are far more familiar with the heinous shit that Catholicism and Protestantism have pulled over the ages than with the darker side of, say, Buddhism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1024-thesaurasaurus","name":"Thesaurasaurus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:37:37-07:00","id":986355,"title":"","content":"Bringing up Japanese media stuff is an interesting part of the equation. For cultural reasons, very few anime, manga, etc. are explicitly Christian. While this most frequently leads to a lot of widely inaccurate portrayals of Christianity, a lot of the material still paints Christianity in the positive. Though reactions can vary widely since it can often come off as very superficial (that is the trappings of Christianity are shown in a positive light but there is no depth towards Christian beliefs), and indulging in the exotic.<br /> <br /> There's a good number of works that deal with the early period of Christianity in Japan (ex: Rurouni Kenshin and Samurai Champloo), where Japanese Christians aren't the protagonists, but are shown in a highly sympathetic light due to the incredibly harsh persecutions of them at the time. Kenshin is an interesting example since the main character's story is extremely similar to many stories of Christian converts struggling as they attempt to reject their former lives and find solace in teachings that promote peace, harmony, and tolerance.<br /> <br /> There are explicit subtext parables as well. The most well known is probably Trigun. While most people tend to focus on Wolfwood and his not-really-Christian thing, Vash himself is very much supposed to promote Christian ideals by being a technical pacifist, promoting peace and love, and constantly willing to suffer for the sake of others.<br /> <br /> And we have examples of completely unsubtle things like Blue Exorcist. It suffers a lot from the &quot;getting Christianity completely wrong&quot; thing, But at the same time, the Church in the story manages to pull off one of the greatest conversion/redemption feats imaginable in Christian theology: getting the explicit antichrist to choose working for the Church to protect the world from his father instead of helping his father defeat good once and for all. &quot;Even the antichrist can find the light of Jesus by being shown true loving kindness,&quot; is a pretty pro-Christian message to me.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:50:30-07:00","id":986361,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986332#post986332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say, even if he isn't explicitly stated as such, Peter Parker (Spider-Man) tends to be a good Protestant boy.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is pretty common in a lot of comic book superheroes even as comics expand to include things that make holding on to real -world belief systems hard.<br /> <br /> Matthew Murdock/Daredevil is a practicing Catholic and his faith is an essential part of his moral code. Steve Rodgers/Captain America, and Clark Kent/Superman are both similar to Peter Parker as being not terribly religious but holding strongly to mainstream US Protestant morality. Kurt Wagner/Nightcrawler is another character frequently portrayed as being driven towards heroics in part due to being Catholic. John Constantine is a shitty Christian, but a lot of Hellblazer's supporting cast include positive Christian characters that try to get John to at least be a bit less of a dick.<br /> <br /> Heck even Spawn, which is one of the number of comics from the 90s/00s that heavily indulges in heavily subverting Christianity and painting Heaven and Hell as equally bad forces, has the most moral and positive character be Spawn's ex-mother-in-law, a devout Christian (I don't think they specify what sect). For at least the first 1/3 of the comics she's the only character that can get through to Spawn and get him to walk a better path that Hell has in plan for him, because she's pretty much the only character that has any faith that there's still good in him.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:57:01-07:00","id":986367,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986361#post986361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is pretty common in a lot of comic book superheroes even as comics expand to include things that make holding on to real -world belief systems hard.<br /> <br /> Matthew Murdock/Daredevil is a practicing Catholic and his faith is an essential part of his moral code. Steve Rodgers/Captain America, and Clark Kent/Superman are both similar to Peter Parker as being not terribly religious but holding strongly to mainstream US Protestant morality. Kurt Wagner/Nightcrawler is another character frequently portrayed as being driven towards heroics in part due to being Catholic. John Constantine is a shitty Christian, but a lot of Hellblazer's supporting cast include positive Christian characters that try to get John to at least be a bit less of a dick.<br /> <br /> Heck even Spawn, which is one of the number of comics from the 90s/00s that heavily indulges in heavily subverting Christianity and painting Heaven and Hell as equally bad forces, has the most moral and positive character be Spawn's ex-mother-in-law, a devout Christian (I don't think they specify what sect). For at least the first 1/3 of the comics she's the only character that can get through to Spawn and get him to walk a better path that Hell has in plan for him, because she's pretty much the only character that has any faith that there's still good in him.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good call on John Constantine and Nightcrawler. I know the Walking Dead has a few positive Christian characters as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T13:59:10-07:00","id":986368,"title":"","content":"There's also the second season (Asylum) of American Horror Story. It's a terrible, TERRIBLE show, but demons are seen as a real and undeniable force of evil, and a number of the nuns are represented as well-intentioned.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T14:02:17-07:00","id":986369,"title":"","content":"When the Christian sensibilities of a character or entire work of fiction are implicit, what is it one uses to distinguish between sects?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T14:05:09-07:00","id":986371,"title":"","content":"Most comics portray Christianity and Catholicism in a good light, while still pointing out that bad people can be religious too. Nightcrawler and Rahne Sinclaire, Wolfsbane, for existence, are both religious but have had formative bad experiences with individuals of their faith. Helena Bertinelli's Huntress had Catholicism as a major aspect of their character, and obviously you have things like the Spectre and Zauriel who was an angel and member of the Justice League after falling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T14:12:41-07:00","id":986376,"title":"","content":"At the mention of <i>Left Behind</i>, I'm obligated to link to the venerable (and ongoing) decade long reviews by Fred Clark. Bad theology = bad fiction.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.patheos.com/blogs/slacktivist/2011/08/07/left-behind-index-i-posts-1-50/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.patheos.com/blogs/slackti...-i-posts-1-50/</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T14:22:19-07:00","id":986379,"title":"","content":"Constantine - the Movie version is especially kind to Catholicism, and all of the Catholic rites have real power. The comic series is more focused on the occult, but it still presents Christianity as 'correct' in my experience with it.<br /> <br /> Lucifer/The Sandman Series - Same continuity, and God is portrayed sympathetically in both.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11726-solana","name":"Solana","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11726_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"342"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T14:29:18-07:00","id":986383,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986361#post986361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is pretty common in a lot of comic book superheroes even as comics expand to include things that make holding on to real -world belief systems hard.<br /> <br /> Matthew Murdock/Daredevil is a practicing Catholic and his faith is an essential part of his moral code. Steve Rodgers/Captain America, and Clark Kent/Superman are both similar to Peter Parker as being not terribly religious but holding strongly to mainstream US Protestant morality. Kurt Wagner/Nightcrawler is another character frequently portrayed as being driven towards heroics in part due to being Catholic. John Constantine is a shitty Christian, but a lot of Hellblazer's supporting cast include positive Christian characters that try to get John to at least be a bit less of a dick.<br /> <br /> Heck even Spawn, which is one of the number of comics from the 90s/00s that heavily indulges in heavily subverting Christianity and painting Heaven and Hell as equally bad forces, has the most moral and positive character be Spawn's ex-mother-in-law, a devout Christian (I don't think they specify what sect). For at least the first 1/3 of the comics she's the only character that can get through to Spawn and get him to walk a better path that Hell has in plan for him, because she's pretty much the only character that has any faith that there's still good in him.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Someone already beat me to my examples :c<br /> <br /> Unfortunately I'm not the type to purposefully seek out fiction of this nature, so it's not really something I'm conscious of in most cases.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11726-solana","name":"Solana","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11726_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"342"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T14:38:20-07:00","id":986391,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wonderandawe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction#post986235\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It was mentioned in the thread linked above that Christianity is one of Genre fiction's favorite whipping boy. Now we could continue to chew that topic to death or focus on positive portrayals of Christianity in Sci-Fi and Fantasy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Does anyone think that's true? Most examples of religious bad guys are examples of extremists, or how one person can co-opt a social organization for their own benefit, or people focusing on one point because of a personal bias and not the tenets of the religion at large.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-12-2016, 05:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T15:06:47-07:00","id":986416,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure it's 100% accurate, but in the West, Christianity gets stuck as the stand in for all traditional organized religion in a lot of fiction. So if an author/creator has issue with organized religion has a whole, Christianity or an obvious analog is frequently used as a corrupt organization with a few good apples in it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T15:12:57-07:00","id":986425,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wonderandawe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986293#post986293\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I never read the book and just looked at the wikipedia article. Considering the pope in the book was raptured because &quot;he came to see the truth in the teachings of Martian Luther&quot;, I'll leave it off the list.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yup. Not friendly if you're Catholic. Or Non-American, since the Rapture's almost exclusively an American Protestant phenomenon.<br /> <br /> I sure hope he wasn't reading Martin's <i>On the Jews and their Lies </i>or <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vom_Schem_Hamphoras\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0b0080\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>Vom Schem Hamphoras</i></span></span></span></a> or <i>Warnings About the Jews.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T15:19:08-07:00","id":986429,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jachra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986425#post986425\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I sure hope he wasn't reading Martin's <i>On the Jews and their Lies </i>or <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vom_Schem_Hamphoras\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0b0080\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>Vom Schem Hamphoras</i></span></span></span></a> or <i>Warnings About the Jews.</i></div> </div> </div> </div>Speaking of which, in thinking about religious comic book characters I noted a few who were Jewish. Kitty Pryde, Katherine Kane, Ragman. But I can't think of any time when Jewish faith was portrayed as a positive thing in itself. Obviously a lot of fiction, especially in comics, portray Nazi-beating, but 'they shouldn't all be killed' isn't a ringing endorsement for the faith itself. Usually Christian characters go to church or someplace and we're shown how their faith helps them deal with situations, or 'do the right thing' in others but not with Judaism.<br /> <br /> That said, I haven't read Ragman so maybe it's there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T15:25:03-07:00","id":986433,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986429#post986429\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Speaking of which, in thinking about religious comic book characters I noted a few who were Jewish. Kitty Pryde, Katherine Kane, Ragman. But I can't think of any time when Jewish faith was portrayed as a positive thing in itself. Obviously a lot of fiction, especially in comics, portray Nazi-beating, but 'they shouldn't all be killed' isn't a ringing endorsement for the faith itself. Usually Christian characters go to church or someplace and we're shown how their faith helps them deal with situations, or 'do the right thing' in others but not with Judaism.<br /> <br /> That said, I haven't read Ragman so maybe it's there.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually an interesting question. Judaism tends to be a thing characters have in genre fiction, but it rarely comes up systematically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T15:47:34-07:00","id":986441,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986429#post986429\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Speaking of which, in thinking about religious comic book characters I noted a few who were Jewish. Kitty Pryde, Katherine Kane, Ragman. But I can't think of any time when Jewish faith was portrayed as a positive thing in itself. Obviously a lot of fiction, especially in comics, portray Nazi-beating, but 'they shouldn't all be killed' isn't a ringing endorsement for the faith itself. Usually Christian characters go to church or someplace and we're shown how their faith helps them deal with situations, or 'do the right thing' in others but not with Judaism.<br /> <br /> That said, I haven't read Ragman so maybe it's there.</div> </div> </div> </div>And let's not forget the bookends on the scale of &quot;obvious&quot; to &quot;he's a WHAT?!?&quot; Judaism: Magneto and Ben Grimm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T15:57:59-07:00","id":986446,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986429#post986429\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Speaking of which, in thinking about religious comic book characters I noted a few who were Jewish. Kitty Pryde, Katherine Kane, Ragman. But I can't think of any time when Jewish faith was portrayed as a positive thing in itself. Obviously a lot of fiction, especially in comics, portray Nazi-beating, but 'they shouldn't all be killed' isn't a ringing endorsement for the faith itself. Usually Christian characters go to church or someplace and we're shown how their faith helps them deal with situations, or 'do the right thing' in others but not with Judaism.<br /> <br /> That said, I haven't read Ragman so maybe it's there.</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe Ben Grimm is also Jewish.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T16:46:39-07:00","id":986469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986446#post986446\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I believe Ben Grimm is also Jewish.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah. He's canonically Jewish. He was largely a stand-in for Jack Kirby.<br /> <br /> Kate Kane/Batwoman is also portrayed as a practicing Jew, and there's been some work done in presenting Superman as an allegory for Moses (mostly by Grant Morrison).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8492,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","user":"dxanders","date":". 10-12-2016, 07:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T18:05:56-07:00","id":986500,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>dxanders</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986469#post986469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">there's been some work done in presenting Superman as an allegory for Moses (mostly by Grant Morrison).</div> </div> </div> </div>I think Grant Morrison has stated that Superman should be written similar to Jesus, not Moses. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T18:09:07-07:00","id":986502,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fat Larry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986500#post986500\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think Grant Morrison has stated that Superman should be written similar to Jesus, not Moses.</div> </div> </div> </div>He's played both angles. Morrison's All-Star Superman was akin to a Jesus figure, but he also built the metaphor of baby Kal-El as baby Moses on the river in his Action Comics run.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8492,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","user":"dxanders","date":". 10-12-2016, 09:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T18:41:56-07:00","id":986510,"title":"","content":"I don't want to derail this thread I suggested to avoid derailing a different thread, but to address the Judaism thing:<br /> <br /> There's a lot of factors that go into why Judiasm isn't something you see as its own entity beyond Jewish characters.<br /> <br /> First, Judaism is an incredibly disorganized formal religion. There's no equivalent to the Pope, or even the Catholic Church, that acts as a centralized body that has a large degree of (at least social) authority on Jewish orthodoxy. Despite having Reform Judaism as a thing, there's no such thing as Protestant Judaism. Reform Jews reject traditional orthodox rulings or dogma, but aren't rejecting any specific body of Jewish authorities. There are, of course, heated debates between different sects of Judaism, but it takes a very different form.<br /> <br /> Second, Jewish religious bodies have evolved over a very long period of time to be a mixture of shy, elitist, and secretive (side note: see where the word &quot;cabal&quot; comes from). There's a certainly a pressure to have a public face and a private face for most Jewish religious bodies. Knowing a lot about the private side of things makes it hard to turn that into pop-culture media without disrespecting why such things are seen as private matters in the first place; but not including them reduces the authenticity of any attempts to work Judaism as more than a personal character trait.<br /> <br /> Third, Judaism is very small. A piece of fiction putting forth Judaism as a positive force (either directly or in allegory) that isn't targeted towards Jews faces a pretty big lift before most people aren't going to get it. People don't want to have to read a primer on Judaism that's almost as long as a novel in order to actually get the point, but there's just a lot of things people don't know unless they spend a significant amount of time studying Judaism. The 2012 film The Possession is an excellent example of how trying to do a Jewish version of things ends up not working. I actually like the film, but ultimately it's an Exorcist knock off with Hebrew instead of Latin instead of at look a Jewish concepts of possession and exorcism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T21:15:21-07:00","id":986569,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solana</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986379#post986379\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Constantine - the Movie version is especially kind to Catholicism, and all of the Catholic rites have real power. The comic series is more focused on the occult, but it still presents Christianity as 'correct' in my experience with it.<br /> <br /> Lucifer/The Sandman Series - Same continuity, and God is portrayed sympathetically in both.</div> </div> </div> </div>As far as Sandman goes, I'm particularly reminded of <a href=\"http://imgur.com/RFTVylF\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> scene with Duma, the angel of silence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4578-zale","name":"Zale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4578_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"156"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-12T21:24:55-07:00","id":986571,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zale</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986569#post986569\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> As far as Sandman goes, I'm particularly reminded of <a href=\"http://imgur.com/RFTVylF\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> scene with Duma, the angel of silence.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Neil Gaiman is definitely a poet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11726-solana","name":"Solana","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11726_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"342"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T10:31:34-07:00","id":986831,"title":"","content":"I've always viewed Superman has more of a Tzadik HaDor figure than a strictly Jesus figure. That being said, the character is meant to be elemental and to absorb the projections of his readers, so a certain Christ-ifying was probably inevitable existing in the American cultural milieu. Christianity is sticky like that, gets on everything and you'll never scrub it out of the carpet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T10:45:38-07:00","id":986842,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986831#post986831\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've always viewed Superman has more of a Tzadik HaDor figure than a strictly Jesus figure. That being said, the character is meant to be elemental and to absorb the projections of his readers, so a certain Christ-ifying was probably inevitable existing in the American cultural milieu. Christianity is sticky like that, gets on everything and you'll never scrub it out of the carpet.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, this. I have difficulty counting positive portrayals of individual Christians as positive portrayals of Christianity for this reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T11:19:23-07:00","id":986856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=986831#post986831\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Christianity is sticky like that, gets on everything and you'll never scrub it out of the carpet.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm going to make this into a bumper sticker.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T12:30:02-07:00","id":986892,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://www.daughterofthelilies.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Daughter of the Lilies</a> is a webcomic that is &quot;largely about the importance self-worth, the different forms love can take, and issues relating to anxiety. (There are also unicorns, manticores, ghouls, goblins, cannibalistic elves, and so on.) &quot; And that the author has mentioned is an attempt at a positive portrayal of christian values in a fantasy webcomic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When one is accustomed to privilege, equality seems like oppression.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7195-tidygamer","name":"TidyGamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7195_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"644"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T12:53:01-07:00","id":986902,"title":"","content":"Does anyone associate Warhammer 40k's Imperial Cult enough with real world religions to think of it as a negative portrayal of them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T13:04:15-07:00","id":986905,"title":"","content":"Like every single thing in Warhammer, everything is so stylized and overblown that I can't take it seriously as a reflection of anything in the real world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When one is accustomed to privilege, equality seems like oppression.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7195-tidygamer","name":"TidyGamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7195_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"644"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T14:01:42-07:00","id":986940,"title":"","content":"On top of what TidyGamer said, if it resembles anything is Roman tendency to deify their leaders (Divus Julius)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T14:28:58-07:00","id":986954,"title":"","content":"Yeah, Warhammer 40k's Imperial Cult would be more similar to various Roman, Egyptian, or Japanese religious views that deified their leader as a living god. It doesn't really feel like it touches on most modern religions in any particular way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T22:13:50-07:00","id":987099,"title":"","content":"Curiosity here, do you need Christianity to be portrayed well or even in your genre-fiction to find it enjoyable?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>pls respont","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/372-cerealkiller","name":"cerealkiller","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar372_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"340"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T23:27:36-07:00","id":987117,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>cerealkiller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=987099#post987099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Curiosity here, do you need Christianity to be portrayed well or even in your genre-fiction to find it enjoyable?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd assume people would want things that they identify with to be portrayed well, be the thing in question their religion, their race, their sexuality or something else. I know that I have a catholic friend who gets pretty upset when media portrays all catholics as basically wizards. I know it upsets me when people portray atheists as hardcore militant neckbeards.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When one is accustomed to privilege, equality seems like oppression.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7195-tidygamer","name":"TidyGamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7195_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"644"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-13T23:34:41-07:00","id":987118,"title":"","content":"Well, it is the objective of this thread, at least. It seems is more diffficult to find positive representations than not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T01:17:25-07:00","id":987146,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>cerealkiller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=987099#post987099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Curiosity here, do you need Christianity to be portrayed well or even in your genre-fiction to find it enjoyable?</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a spin off from a thread with a poster that was complaining about pop-culture being too anti-Christian, so that's some of the context of why this came up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thorbes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=987118#post987118\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, it is the objective of this thread, at least. It seems is more diffficult to find positive representations than not.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't say that's really the case, largely because the thread has cut out the fairly large amount of material that is overtly targeting Christians as the primary market rather than more general stuff. As well, there's a lot of Western media where the default assumption of &quot;Christian&quot; is built into character values, but it's such a norm that we don't really consider it to be &quot;pro-Christian&quot; (see the Peter Parker example).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T04:07:58-07:00","id":987179,"title":"","content":"I invite to go to the wiki of the superheroes marvel and DC, and see &quot;Jew characters&quot;, &quot;LGBT characters&quot; and &quot;Catholic characters&quot;.<br /> <br /> Amakusa Sayo, sister of the antagonist Amakusha Shogo, from the anime &quot;Ruroni Kenshi&quot;. For me it was a surprise.<br /> <br /> The nun from &quot;Silent Hill: Douwnpour&quot;.<br /> <br /> Do you know the novel &quot;the father Elijah&quot; by Michael O'brien? Let's say it's like the anti-Dan Brown works.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Tell me what you brag and I will tell you what you lack&quot; (Spanish saying)<br /> <br /> \u00abThe Master Confucius said: \u201cThe noble man is in harmony but does not follow the crowd. The inferior man follows the crowd, but is not in harmony\u201d\u00bb (Anaclet 13:23)","edited":{"userid":11152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11152-luis-carlos","user":"Luis Carlos","date":". 10-14-2016, 07:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11152-luis-carlos","name":"Luis Carlos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"258"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T07:02:54-07:00","id":987236,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>cerealkiller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=987099#post987099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Curiosity here, do you need Christianity to be portrayed well or even in your genre-fiction to find it enjoyable?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Personally, no. As Heavy Arms said, the thread linked in the first post when off topic and there were requests to start a new thread about discussing Christianity in pop culture. I decided to bite the bullet and start a thread on the topic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T07:07:10-07:00","id":987238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/986235-positive-portrayals-of-christianity-in-genre-fiction?p=987146#post987146\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wouldn't say that's really the case, largely because the thread has cut out the fairly large amount of material that is overtly targeting Christians as the primary market rather than more general stuff. As well, there's a lot of Western media where the default assumption of &quot;Christian&quot; is built into character values, but it's such a norm that we don't really consider it to be &quot;pro-Christian&quot; (see the Peter Parker example).</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't really mean to cut off Christian Literature from this thread. I am just unfamiliar with that genre and was unable to supply examples. <br /> <br /> When I was a kid, there was a show called &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Father_Dowling_Mysteries\" target=\"_blank\">Father Dowling's Mysteries</a>&quot;. It was a priest and a nun who solved crimes. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T22:22:02-07:00","id":987618,"title":"","content":"In a series that's basically the Dirty Dozen with Supervillains, the Priest here was one of the sanest, sensible and helpful members of the supporting cast.<br /> <img src=\"https://chasemagnett.files.wordpress.com/2016/08/suicide-squad-personal-files-189148.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Me